{"reason": "The initial plan identified that freezing ModernBERT backbone limits performance. Historical experiments show that progressive unfreezing and data augmentation are successful strategies for improving fine-tuning of large transformers on small text datasets. The current validation log-loss of 0.475 is close to the logistic regression baseline of 0.452, confirming the frozen backbone is not leveraging the model's full capacity. Therefore, modifying model_design to enable progressive backbone unfreezing and data_processing_and_feature_engineering to add text augmentation are the most promising changes. These directly address the root cause and are informed by similar successful experiments.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add a collate function in the DataLoader that randomly masks 15% of tokens (replacing with [MASK] token id) and with 0.5 probability swaps adjacent sentences if multiple sentences exist. This augmentation is applied only to training data, not validation or test. The function should be passed as collate_fn to the DataLoader. This forces the model to rely on stylistic context and improves robustness against overfitting, directly addressing the limited training data and the gap between training and validation loss.", "model_design": "Modify the model to support progressive unfreezing of the ModernBERT backbone. Implement a method unfreeze_stage(stage) where stage 0: freeze backbone (only head trainable) for epochs 1-2; stage 1: unfreeze last 8 transformer layers for epochs 3-5; stage 2: unfreeze entire backbone for epochs 6-10. Adjust learning rates per stage: head lr=3e-5 (stage 0), backbone lr=1e-5 (stage 1), backbone lr=5e-6 (stage 2). This allows gradual adaptation of pretrained representations to author-specific patterns without catastrophic forgetting, as validated in similar historical experiments."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan identified that freezing ModernBERT backbone limits performance. Historical experiments show that progressive unfreezing and data augmentation are successful strategies for improving fine-tuning of large transformers on small text datasets. The current validation log-loss of 0.475 is close to the logistic regression baseline of 0.452, confirming the frozen backbone is not leveraging the model's full capacity. Therefore, modifying model_design to enable progressive backbone unfreezing and data_processing_and_feature_engineering to add text augmentation are the most promising changes. These directly address the root cause and are informed by similar successful experiments.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add a collate function in the DataLoader that randomly masks 15% of tokens (replacing with [MASK] token id) and with 0.5 probability swaps adjacent sentences if multiple sentences exist. This augmentation is applied only to training data, not validation or test. The function should be passed as collate_fn to the DataLoader. This forces the model to rely on stylistic context and improves robustness against overfitting, directly addressing the limited training data and the gap between training and validation loss.",
    "model_design": "Modify the model to support progressive unfreezing of the ModernBERT backbone. Implement a method unfreeze_stage(stage) where stage 0: freeze backbone (only head trainable) for epochs 1-2; stage 1: unfreeze last 8 transformer layers for epochs 3-5; stage 2: unfreeze entire backbone for epochs 6-10. Adjust learning rates per stage: head lr=3e-5 (stage 0), backbone lr=1e-5 (stage 1), backbone lr=5e-6 (stage 2). This allows gradual adaptation of pretrained representations to author-specific patterns without catastrophic forgetting, as validated in similar historical experiments."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting: training loss drops to near zero while validation loss increases after epoch 2, exactly as described in the initial plan. The best validation log loss is 0.3205, which is only slightly better than the LR baseline. The initial plan proposed EMA and increased dropout to combat overfitting. Similar historical experiments successfully used EMA averaging and higher dropout to improve generalization. These changes directly address the root cause (overfitting) and can be implemented within the 'training_evaluation' module. No changes to data processing or model architecture are needed as the feature engineering and base model are already effective.", "module": ["training_evaluation"], "plan": {"training_evaluation": "Add exponential moving average (EMA) of model parameters: after each training epoch, update an EMA copy of the model's state_dict using decay=0.999. At the end of training, replace the model weights with EMA weights for validation and test inference. Also, increase hidden_dropout_prob from 0.1 to 0.3 and attention_probs_dropout_prob from 0.1 to 0.2 in the model configuration. These changes will reduce overfitting by smoothing weight updates and forcing more robust feature learning, targeting the training loss collapse and validation loss degradation observed in the execution. Implementation: modify the training loop to maintain EMA parameters, increase dropout values in config, and ensure model.eval() uses EMA weights."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting: training loss drops to near zero while validation loss increases after epoch 2, exactly as described in the initial plan. The best validation log loss is 0.3205, which is only slightly better than the LR baseline. The initial plan proposed EMA and increased dropout to combat overfitting. Similar historical experiments successfully used EMA averaging and higher dropout to improve generalization. These changes directly address the root cause (overfitting) and can be implemented within the 'training_evaluation' module. No changes to data processing or model architecture are needed as the feature engineering and base model are already effective.",
  "module": ["training_evaluation"],
