    "training_evaluation": "Add exponential moving average (EMA) of model parameters: after each training epoch, update an EMA copy of the model's state_dict using decay=0.999. At the end of training, replace the model weights with EMA weights for validation and test inference. Also, increase hidden_dropout_prob from 0.1 to 0.3 and attention_probs_dropout_prob from 0.1 to 0.2 in the model configuration. These changes will reduce overfitting by smoothing weight updates and forcing more robust feature learning, targeting the training loss collapse and validation loss degradation observed in the execution. Implementation: modify the training loop to maintain EMA parameters, increase dropout values in config, and ensure model.eval() uses EMA weights."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution shows severe overfitting (train loss 0.0002, val loss 0.5427), confirming the initial plan's diagnosis. Similar historical experiments showed that EMA implementation errors (double application, too aggressive decay) caused performance degradation. The successful experiments used gradient accumulation, layer-wise learning rate decay, and mixup augmentation. Therefore, I focus on training_evaluation to implement these three changes, and model_design to adjust layer-wise decay groups. Data processing remains unchanged as it already provides good feature engineering.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "WHAT: Define parameter groups for layer-wise learning rate decay. Group parameters into three tiers based on layer index: lower layers (0-10) with 0.5x base LR, middle layers (11-21) with 0.8x, and upper layers (22-31) plus classifier with 1.0x. Use layer names from ModernBertForSequenceClassification to identify layer indices. WHY: Historical experiments showed that uniform learning across 32 layers causes catastrophic forgetting of pretrained features; lower layers need less perturbation. HOW: Create a function to assign layer index to each parameter name, then feed separate parameter groups to AdamW optimizer. Interface: Ensure optimizer_grouped_params variable is updated with three tiered groups instead of original two weight decay groups.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Three changes: (1) Replace EMA training with gradient accumulation (accumulation_steps=4) and increase epochs to 8. Remove all EMA code. (2) Increase weight decay from 0.01 to 0.1. (3) Implement mixup augmentation: sample lambda ~ Beta(0.5,0.5) per batch, mix input_ids and labels between current batch and a permuted version. Use the mixed input_ids (as float tensor, then embed) and soft labels for cross-entropy. WHY: Historical EMA experiments failed due to double application and aggressive decay; gradient accumulation stabilizes gradients and naturally regularizes. Higher weight decay penalizes large weights. Mixup creates interpolated training examples, forcing smooth decision boundaries crucial for author style differentiation. HOW: In training loop, after getting a batch, create permuted indices, compute lambda, mix input_ids (via embedding lookup) and labels, then proceed with forward pass and loss computation. Accumulate gradients over 4 steps before optimizer.step(). Interface: Keep all data loaders, dataloader parameters, and evaluation loop unchanged. The submission generation remains identical."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows severe overfitting (train loss 0.0002, val loss 0.5427), confirming the initial plan's diagnosis. Similar historical experiments showed that EMA implementation errors (double application, too aggressive decay) caused performance degradation. The successful experiments used gradient accumulation, layer-wise learning rate decay, and mixup augmentation. Therefore, I focus on training_evaluation to implement these three changes, and model_design to adjust layer-wise decay groups. Data processing remains unchanged as it already provides good feature engineering.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "WHAT: Define parameter groups for layer-wise learning rate decay. Group parameters into three tiers based on layer index: lower layers (0-10) with 0.5x base LR, middle layers (11-21) with 0.8x, and upper layers (22-31) plus classifier with 1.0x. Use layer names from ModernBertForSequenceClassification to identify layer indices. WHY: Historical experiments showed that uniform learning across 32 layers causes catastrophic forgetting of pretrained features; lower layers need less perturbation. HOW: Create a function to assign layer index to each parameter name, then feed separate parameter groups to AdamW optimizer. Interface: Ensure optimizer_grouped_params variable is updated with three tiered groups instead of original two weight decay groups.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Three changes: (1) Replace EMA training with gradient accumulation (accumulation_steps=4) and increase epochs to 8. Remove all EMA code. (2) Increase weight decay from 0.01 to 0.1. (3) Implement mixup augmentation: sample lambda ~ Beta(0.5,0.5) per batch, mix input_ids and labels between current batch and a permuted version. Use the mixed input_ids (as float tensor, then embed) and soft labels for cross-entropy. WHY: Historical EMA experiments failed due to double application and aggressive decay; gradient accumulation stabilizes gradients and naturally regularizes. Higher weight decay penalizes large weights. Mixup creates interpolated training examples, forcing smooth decision boundaries crucial for author style differentiation. HOW: In training loop, after getting a batch, create permuted indices, compute lambda, mix input_ids (via embedding lookup) and labels, then proceed with forward pass and loss computation. Accumulate gradients over 4 steps before optimizer.step(). Interface: Keep all data loaders, dataloader parameters, and evaluation loop unchanged. The submission generation remains identical."
