{"reason": "The initial plan identifies overfitting as the main issue, with a validation log loss of 0.326 and training loss of 0.067. Similar historical experiments show that FGM adversarial training and text augmentation effectively regularize ModernBERT-based classifiers. Additionally, historical failures highlight the importance of correct EMA implementation (not double-applying and using appropriate decay). To stay true to the plan while addressing these issues: 1) data_processing_and_feature_engineering is needed to implement text augmentation before tokenization, 2) training_evaluation is needed to integrate FGM and ensure correct EMA handling (lower decay, save before EMA). Model design remains optimal and unchanged per the plan.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add a text augmentation function (SSMix-inspired) that performs synonym replacement and random word insertion on 15% of tokens per sample using a static synonym dictionary. Integrate this function into the AuthorDataset's __getitem__ method: after retrieving the text, apply augmentation with 50% probability, then tokenize. This stays true to the initial plan's goal of improving generalization via lexical variation, and aligns with successful historical experiments that used mixup augmentation for text. The interface (Dataset returning input_ids, attention_mask, labels) remains unchanged.", "training_evaluation": "Implement two modifications: 1) Add Fast Gradient Method (FGM) adversarial training during the backward pass when the backbone is unfrozen (stages 1 and 2). After computing the standard loss, perturb the embedding layer (model.backbone.embeddings.tok_embeddings) with epsilon=0.5, re-run forward with perturbed embeddings in no_grad mode to compute adversarial loss, and add it to the original loss. 2) Fix EMA implementation: reduce decay from 0.995 to 0.9995? Actually, historical successful experiments used 0.995, so keep decay=0.995 but ensure EMA is applied correctly: save checkpoint before applying shadow at epoch end, and when applying shadow for inference, do not re-apply EMA again after loading. Also, re-register EMA after each stage transition to include newly unfrozen parameters. This directly addresses the overfitting noted in the initial plan and avoids pitfalls from historical failures."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan identifies overfitting as the main issue, with a validation log loss of 0.326 and training loss of 0.067. Similar historical experiments show that FGM adversarial training and text augmentation effectively regularize ModernBERT-based classifiers. Additionally, historical failures highlight the importance of correct EMA implementation (not double-applying and using appropriate decay). To stay true to the plan while addressing these issues: 1) data_processing_and_feature_engineering is needed to implement text augmentation before tokenization, 2) training_evaluation is needed to integrate FGM and ensure correct EMA handling (lower decay, save before EMA). Model design remains optimal and unchanged per the plan.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add a text augmentation function (SSMix-inspired) that performs synonym replacement and random word insertion on 15% of tokens per sample using a static synonym dictionary. Integrate this function into the AuthorDataset's __getitem__ method: after retrieving the text, apply augmentation with 50% probability, then tokenize. This stays true to the initial plan's goal of improving generalization via lexical variation, and aligns with successful historical experiments that used mixup augmentation for text. The interface (Dataset returning input_ids, attention_mask, labels) remains unchanged.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement two modifications: 1) Add Fast Gradient Method (FGM) adversarial training during the backward pass when the backbone is unfrozen (stages 1 and 2). After computing the standard loss, perturb the embedding layer (model.backbone.embeddings.tok_embeddings) with epsilon=0.5, re-run forward with perturbed embeddings in no_grad mode to compute adversarial loss, and add it to the original loss. 2) Fix EMA implementation: reduce decay from 0.995 to 0.9995? Actually, historical successful experiments used 0.995, so keep decay=0.995 but ensure EMA is applied correctly: save checkpoint before applying shadow at epoch end, and when applying shadow for inference, do not re-apply EMA again after loading. Also, re-register EMA after each stage transition to include newly unfrozen parameters. This directly addresses the overfitting noted in the initial plan and avoids pitfalls from historical failures."
  }
